<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ion Inculeț</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1884">1884</a>–<a href="/years/1940">1940</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ion Inculeț was a Bessarabian physicist and politician who presided over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sfatul-tarii">Sfatul Țării</a>, the council that proclaimed the Moldavian Democratic Republic in December 1917. As its president he oversaw the council's vote of April 1918 for union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. He later served as a minister in several Romanian governments in the interwar period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ion-inculet.jpg" alt="Ion Inculeț" width="320" height="455" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ion_C._Incule%C5%A3_(1884-1940).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ion Inculeț was born on 5 April 1884 in the village of Răzeni to Constantin and Maria Inculeț, who were peasants; the family later moved to Căinări and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chisinau">Chișinău</a>. He studied at the Theological School and the Theological Seminary in Chișinău, graduating in June 1905, and went on to study natural science at Dorpat University. He wrote for Basarabia, the first Romanian-language newspaper in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a>, under the pseudonym Ion Gându, until the Okhrana raided its offices in February 1907.</p><p>He then moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>, where he taught mathematics at several schools, lectured in mathematics and astronomy as a Privatdozent, and wrote on physics and meteorology. After gaining his doctorate in 1915 he worked as a physicist at the Meteorological Observatory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the February Revolution Inculeț joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socialist-revolutionary-party">Socialist Revolutionary Party</a> and was elected to the Petrograd Soviet. He organised a delegation to explain the revolution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a>, arrived there in August 1917 and was appointed Assistant Government Commissioner. In November 1917 he was elected president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sfatul-tarii">Sfatul Țării</a> in place of the more senior national activist Ion Pelivan, as a moderate who could hold the new body together and link it to Russian state authority. When the council proclaimed the Moldavian Democratic Republic on 2 December 1917, he was announced as its president.</p><p>After the union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> he became Minister of State for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a> in the government of Marghiloman and argued in the agrarian reform for larger land allotments for peasants. On 23 August 1918 he was among the founders of the Bessarabian Peasants' Party, whose wing under him joined the National Liberal Party in 1923. He was Minister of Health from 1927 to 9 November 1928 and deputy head of the government of Gheorghe Tătărescu from 1933 to 1937.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Elected a full member of the Romanian Academy in 1918, he gave his inaugural speech, Space and time in new scientific light, on Einstein's theory of relativity. He married Princess Roxana Cantacuzino. Inculeț died of a heart attack on 18 November 1940 and was buried at the Bellu Cemetery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a>; on 7 June 1942 his remains and his wife's were moved to Bârnova, near Iași, where their tombs lie in the Church of Saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ion Inculeț” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A regional council, the Sfatul Țării, met in Chișinău and on 2 December 1917 proclaimed the Moldavian Democratic Republic as an autonomous member of a planned Russian federation, with <b>Ion Inculeț</b> as president.</blockquote><a href="/state/moldavian-democratic-republic">Moldavian Democratic Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Răzeni, in what is now <a href="/countries/moldova">Moldova</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.77,28.9&amp;year=1920" aria-label="Răzeni on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ion Inculeț"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4936 6121h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4936 6121h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.77,28.9&amp;year=1920">What was here in 1920? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
